  "updated": "2026-08-19T18:57:39.730231+00:00",
  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry today through tonight.\n\nWith the low earlier this week now off the coast of Nova Scotia, \nhigh pressure has been able to build in over southern New England in \nits place. Notably aloft, a trough well to our north and west in \nCanada continues shifting eastward over the southern portion of the \nHudson Bay. With where we are in context of this trough, strong SW-\nly flow aloft develops today through the column. Diurnal mixing \nshould also aid in mixing down this SW flow, which may inhibit sea \nbreeze development despite the presence of high pressure centered \njust off to our east. Any early morning fog and lower clouds will \ndiminish shortly after sunrise. 925 mb temperatures will likely \nremain in the 20-23C range for the afternoon, encouraging highs into \nthe mid to upper 80s.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Unsettled weather returning Thursday with a front \npassing through, followed by a brief tranquil period Friday.\n\nA shortwave within this slightly SW flow makes its way towards \nsouthern New England heading into tomorrow, bringing with it more \nfavorable dynamics and moisture for showers as the jet aloft also \nbecomes more SW and cyclonic. With better ascent and forcing, will \nneed to keep an eye on where the best moisture tracks. The \ndifferences between the CAMs and the global guidance remain quite \nsignificant, with the NAM showing greater moisture through the \ncolumn across the whole region, while the Canadian, ECMWF, and GFS \nall indicate layers of dry air in the lower to mid levels that would \ngreatly affect precipitation. Given the signal for dry air limiting \nrainfall, I kept the chances for precipitation to around 30% or less \nand total QPF to up to 0.15\". This is in line with what most of the \nensemble guidance is indicating as well, as the greater \nconcentration of moisture is shown in this guidance to remain just \nto our southwest.\n\nFollowing the passage of this front, much drier air files in behind \nit. Dew points are expected to drop into the 50s across the region \nand remain generally in the upper 50s and low 60s Thursday night \ninto Friday. High pressure makes its return Friday as a brief \nrespite from this unsettled pattern, with onshore breezes and highs \nin the low 80s and upper 70s, with mid 70s along the immediate \ncoastal areas.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Another round of unsettled weather possible for the \nweekend.\n\nA much larger shortwave moves in from the Great Lakes Saturday into \nSunday, greatly amplifying the SW flow aloft. Guidance signals at \nthe possibility of a series of waves tracking NE along within this \nSW flow, and a surface low may pass along the east coast up into \nsouthern New England. Track is still somewhat uncertain, but this \nwould likely bring the return of showers. Depending on the timing of \nthese waves and low pressure, thunderstorms could be possible if \nthey can take advantage of daytime heat.\n\nHeading into the start of next week, it's also worth noting that the \ntroughing associated with the leading shortwave cutting south \nthrough the Great Lakes could end up more as a closed off low aloft. \nDeterministic guidance keys in more on this possibility, but the \nCanadian ensemble notes the possibility along with the ECMWF \nensemble hinting at it slightly; the GEFS keeps it a bit more open \nas of the 00z run. We may find ourselves in the low's post-frontal \nairmass for Monday with more W-ly flow keeping the region slightly \ncooler; 850 mb temps may fall to near 10C. Will be something to \ncontinue monitoring in future updates.",
